1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
QLoRA combines LoRA with a quantised base model so a large model fits on one GPU.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:11,677
The base weights are stored in four bit NormalFloat, NF4, a data type whose levels match a normal distribution of weights.

3
00:00:12,077 --> 00:00:18,577
During the forward pass each block is dequantised to bfloat16 just in time, the LoRA adapters stay in full precision.

4
00:00:18,977 --> 00:00:25,755
Double quantisation also quantises the quantisation constants, saving about half a gigabyte on a sixty-five billion model.

5
00:00:26,155 --> 00:00:33,100
Paged optimisers move optimiser states to CPU memory when the GPU runs out, avoiding out of memory crashes on long sequences.

6
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:38,944
The cost is speed: dequantisation makes training roughly thirty percent slower than bfloat16 LoRA.
